/-
# Irreducible generators of finite-state automorphism groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 16.86)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 16.86 (V. I. Sushchanskiĭ): does the group of
all finite-state automorphisms of a regular rooted tree possess an irreducible system of generators?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.End
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Lattice
public import Mathlib.Basic.Finite.Defs
public import Mathlib.Order.CompletePartialOrder

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FiniteStateIrreducibleGeneratorsKourovka16Problem86Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 16.86 (V. I.
Sushchanskiĭ). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701277 (`KOU-16.86`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The `d`-regular rooted tree (`d ≥ 2`) has the words `List (Fin d)` as vertices. A tree automorphism
is a permutation `g` of the words with `g (v ++ w) = g v ++ u` for a word `u` of the length of `w`;
its section at `v` is the map `w ↦ u`, and `g` is finite-state when it has finitely many distinct
sections. The group is the subgroup of `Equiv.Perm (List (Fin d))` generated by the finite-state
automorphisms, which consists of exactly them. An irreducible system of generators is a generating
set no proper subset of which generates (a minimal generating set, Lavrenyuk's term). The target is
stated for every `d ≥ 2`: Lavrenyuk's regular rooted tree is the tree of words over any finite
alphabet of at least two letters (arXiv:1210.0150, Section 3).
-/

/-- `g` is an automorphism of the `d`-regular rooted tree whose vertices are the words
`List (Fin d)` (the root is `[]`, the children of `v` are the words `v ++ [i]`): for all words
`v, w`, the image of `v ++ w` is the image of `v` followed by a word of the length of `w`. For a
permutation of the words this says that `g` preserves lengths and the prefix order, so it is a
tree automorphism. -/
def IsTreeAutomorphism {d : ℕ} (g : Equiv.Perm (List (Fin d))) : Prop :=
  ∀ v w : List (Fin d), ∃ u : List (Fin d), u.length = w.length ∧ g (v ++ w) = g v ++ u

/-- The section (state) of `g` at the vertex `v`: the map `w ↦ u` with `g (v ++ w) = g v ++ u`. -/
def treeSection {d : ℕ} (g : Equiv.Perm (List (Fin d))) (v : List (Fin d)) :
    List (Fin d) → List (Fin d) :=
  fun w => (g (v ++ w)).drop (g v).length

/-- The group of finite-state automorphisms of the `d`-regular rooted tree: the subgroup of
permutations of the words generated by the tree automorphisms with finitely many distinct
sections. These automorphisms are closed under products and inverses, so the subgroup consists of
exactly them. -/
def finiteStateAutomorphisms (d : ℕ) : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm (List (Fin d))) :=
  Subgroup.closure {g | IsTreeAutomorphism g ∧ (Set.range (treeSection g)).Finite}

/-- Kourovka 16.86 (V. I. Sushchanskiĭ): for every `d ≥ 2` the group of finite-state automorphisms
of the `d`-regular rooted tree has an irreducible system of generators: a generating set none of
whose proper subsets generates the group. -/
def finiteStateAutomorphismsIrreducibleGenerators : Prop :=
  ∀ d : ℕ, 2 ≤ d → ∃ S : Set (finiteStateAutomorphisms d),
    Subgroup.closure S = ⊤ ∧
      ∀ T : Set (finiteStateAutomorphisms d), T ⊂ S → Subgroup.closure T ≠ ⊤

/--
Resolved true: Lavrenyuk (Theorem 3.8) proves that for a regular rooted tree over any alphabet of at
least two letters the group of all finite-state automorphisms has a minimal (irreducible) generating
set; the Kourovka Archive answers 16.86 "Yes, it does". Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov
(eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49
(2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 16.86 (V. I. Sushchanskiĭ); resolution: Ya. Lavrenyuk,
The group of all finite-state automorphisms of a regular rooted tree has a minimal generating set,
Geom. Dedicata 183 (2016), 59-67 (preprint: Minimal generating sets in wreath products,
arXiv:1210.0150), arXiv:1210.0150, Theorem 3.8 (for a regular rooted tree over any alphabet of at
least two letters, the group of finite-state automorphisms has a minimal generating set); Kourovka
Archive, Problem 16.86 (Yes, it does), https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.0150.
-/
public theorem_wanted finiteStateAutomorphismsIrreducibleGenerators_holds :
    finiteStateAutomorphismsIrreducibleGenerators

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FiniteStateIrreducibleGeneratorsKourovka16Problem86Wanted
